   shutdown profit -F = -100; producing q* is better: True
 cases 1 and 4 share p, c and k: True; fixed costs 12 and 100; grid output 4 and 4, equal: True
 case 1 at the minimum-average-cost output sqrt(F/k) = 3.4641: profit 3.7128, below the profit 4.0000 at q*: True</pre>
-        <p class="interpretation">In each of the four cases the grid maximiser equals the closed form, price equals marginal cost there, and producing beats the shutdown profit \(-F\). Case 4 has the same optimal output as case 1 although its profit is lower by 88 (the change in the fixed cost, 100 − 12). The last line computes, in floating point and for case 1 only, whether the profit at the minimum-average-cost output is below the profit at q*; it prints True. So the minimum-average-cost output does not maximize profit: its profit is about 3.71, against 4 at the optimum. The grid is finite, so the lab does not prove optimality for other inputs. Proposition 1.3 does.</p>
+        <p class="interpretation">In each of the four cases the grid maximiser equals the closed form, price equals marginal cost there, and producing beats the shutdown profit \(-F\). Case 4 has the same optimal output as case 1 although its profit is lower by 88 (the change in the fixed cost, 100 − 12). The last line computes, in floating point and for case 1 only, whether the profit at the minimum-average-cost output is below the profit at q*; it prints True. So, in case 1, the minimum-average-cost output does not maximize profit: its profit is about 3.71, against 4 at the optimum. The grid is finite, so the lab does not prove optimality for other inputs. Proposition 1.3 does.</p>
